  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM EST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nBatch of persistently moderate rain showers has slowly nudged \nwestward to a Holland to Kalamazoo line. Radar estimates in excess\nof an inch are common (values are probably slightly inflated due \nto melting layer effects). The forcing is associated with ascent \nat the terminus of the broad deformation axis associated with the \ndeparting cyclone over the Lower Great Lakes. The cyclonic branch \nof the deformation conveyor continues to support a steady moderate\nrain. This forcing is expected to wane in the next couple of \nhours as the flow finds a new balance state, while a second pulse \nof forcing over east-central Lower Michigan takes over for a brief\ntime before also weakening.\n\nThe incoming cold airmass has also activated Lake Michigan. A\nsteady-state northerly flow will maintain the deep lake-effect \nbands well offshore - with exception near Big Sable point, where \nthe primary band will flirt with the shoreline. Current CAM \nguidance wavers - but keeps it offshore most of the night into \nFriday. Otherwise, lake-effect from Lake Huron will try to squeeze\na couple bands toward the southwest that may graze eastern Ingham\nand Jackson Counties, but prospects for any meaningful \nprecipitation in the form of snow are very low.\n\nAs the flow gains a slight west component late Friday and Friday\nnight, the main band structure over Lake Michigan will wander to \nthis side of the lake - likely bringing a rain/snow mix along \nportions of the immediate lakeshore. If high enough intensity is\nmaintained, then a period of all snow may occur - bringing minor\naccumulations to parts of Mason and Oceana Counties.  Additional \nresidual light snow shower activity from the Grand Traverse area \nmay make inroads along and north of US-10.\n\nThe airmass begins to moderate Saturday night and Sunday, \ndeactivating the big lake. The high amplitude upper-level ridge \npoised over the western CONUS will become the dominant weather \ngovernor for a good portion of next week - with mild and quiet \nconditions expected, once again.",
